turned green for you there. So by

01:07:58.887 --> 01:08:02.127
the way, everyone has up to five minutes, just so you know. And then you'll

01:08:02.151 --> 01:08:05.683
hear the chime at the four minute and 30 second mark which gives

01:08:05.699 --> 01:08:09.275
you the 30 second warning. Okay. Don't let it get to five minutes

01:08:09.315 --> 01:08:12.467
because then the microphone gets turned off. Okay. And then stick around just in case

01:08:12.491 --> 01:08:16.175
there's any follow up questions. Okay? Okay. Over to you whenever you're ready.

01:08:17.955 --> 01:08:22.099
Good evening, Mr. Mayor, city councillors, staff and fellow community

01:08:22.187 --> 01:08:25.707
members. My name is Indigo Moran and I am full

01:08:25.731 --> 01:08:29.531
of righteous indignation. On October 31,

01:08:29.643 --> 01:08:33.255
residents of an encampment in St. George's Square were told to leave.

01:08:33.765 --> 01:08:37.533
Eviction notices were placed on their shelters, giving them a deadline of November

01:08:37.589 --> 01:08:41.197
13th. On that day, officials moved in to

01:08:41.221 --> 01:08:45.029
take residents belongings to storage without their consent before even

01:08:45.077 --> 01:08:48.661
10:30 in the morning. These people weren't making money

01:08:48.733 --> 01:08:51.893
by being there. They weren't indirectly taxing people

01:08:51.949 --> 01:08:55.101
and weren't producing excessive amounts of greenhouse gases.

01:08:55.293 --> 01:08:58.581
They were just living there, exercising their right to exist

01:08:58.613 --> 01:09:02.517
in a public space as people who have been ostracized and

01:09:02.541 --> 01:09:05.345
shut out from other places that they may have tried to live.

01:09:05.745 --> 01:09:09.805
They were trying to find community and a semi permanent location to be.

01:09:10.305 --> 01:09:13.633
Yet they were policed, questioned, shunned and eventually

01:09:13.689 --> 01:09:17.273
evicted in spite of the winter being one of the hardest seasons for

01:09:17.329 --> 01:09:20.649
unhoused people, in spite of two premature deaths

01:09:20.697 --> 01:09:24.005
happening just a couple of days prior to this eviction.

01:09:25.425 --> 01:09:28.913
By contrast, Enbridge is having major successes right Now.

01:09:29.049 --> 01:09:32.905
They made 1.3 billion in only the third quarter. This year they're

01:09:32.945 --> 01:09:36.369
expecting to finish 2024 with earnings between 17.7

01:09:36.417 --> 01:09:40.473
and 8.3 billion, all while importing mostly fracked

01:09:40.529 --> 01:09:44.097
gas, according to Environmental Defense Canada for their pipelines

01:09:44.161 --> 01:09:48.161
and emitting abysmal amounts of methane gas, according to UNIFOR

01:09:48.233 --> 01:09:51.409
workers in Queen's Park. Enbridge also ignores leaks in their pipelines

01:09:51.457 --> 01:09:55.041
for weeks, allowing gas to escape. And they're planning to

01:09:55.073 --> 01:09:58.561
stop compliance testing their infrastructure as frequently as they are

01:09:58.593 --> 01:10:02.317
supposed. What is so fundamentally wrong with

01:10:02.341 --> 01:10:06.285
our society that we restrict police and punish people just trying to survive

01:10:06.325 --> 01:10:10.533
in public spaces, yet look the other way when a

01:10:10.549 --> 01:10:14.421
polluting company makes millions from our community through a hidden subsidy.

01:10:14.573 --> 01:10:18.701
Why are we through refusing to address this contradiction, refusing spaces

01:10:18.733 --> 01:10:21.973
to people who have almost nothing and giving money and land to

01:10:21.989 --> 01:10:25.715
a billion dollar corporation by charging them nothing but property taxes?

01:10:27.085 --> 01:10:31.189
Perhaps I and others would be less concerned if $15 million weren't being

01:10:31.237 --> 01:10:34.909
cut mostly from environmental programs next year. Or maybe

01:10:34.957 --> 01:10:39.029
I wouldn't think it was realistic to change our energy consumption if

01:10:39.117 --> 01:10:42.781
this were pre2023 and we didn't know that heat pumps are

01:10:42.813 --> 01:10:46.061
13% cheaper than natural gas. Or if I

01:10:46.093 --> 01:10:49.621
didn't know, sadly, that 83% of

01:10:49.653 --> 01:10:53.445
extreme weather events in the last 20 years have either been

01:10:53.785 --> 01:10:56.965
caused or exacerbated by climate change.

01:10:57.785 --> 01:11:01.605
Or if the youth mental health crisis weren't worsened by climate change,

01:11:01.985 --> 01:11:05.417
with 49% of youth expressing extreme concern

01:11:05.481 --> 01:11:10.049
over the state of our climate. Or even if our healthcare system

01:11:10.097 --> 01:11:13.673
wasn't becoming increasingly privatized. Or if climate doom scrolling

01:11:13.729 --> 01:11:17.233
and political misinformation weren't so rampant in an increasingly

01:11:17.289 --> 01:11:20.623
digital world. But none of those things are

01:11:20.639 --> 01:11:24.071
the case. This is 2024 and we're witnessing

01:11:24.103 --> 01:11:27.927
the climate crisis event effects on weather events and human health.

01:11:28.071 --> 01:11:31.727
We see it through news publications and stories from people we know.

01:11:31.911 --> 01:11:36.327
I've witnessed the impacts of all of this on youth mental health firsthand.

01:11:36.511 --> 01:11:40.527
And we know that it is possible, practical and affordable to

01:11:40.551 --> 01:11:44.127
do better than we are doing. We cannot treat the housing crisis,

01:11:44.231 --> 01:11:47.767
healthcare accessibility, mental health, emergency and the

01:11:47.791 --> 01:11:50.835
climate crisis as isolated separate events.

01:11:51.455 --> 01:11:54.695
We must acknowledge the connections between these issues and work

01:11:54.735 --> 01:11:58.207
to fix them. You can start today by ending

01:11:58.231 --> 01:12:01.111
the subsidization of this fossil gas giant.

01:12:01.303 --> 01:12:04.727
Put that $9 million back in our community and not the pockets of

01:12:04.751 --> 01:12:08.375
a multi billion dollar corporation. Support Councillor

01:12:08.415 --> 01:12:11.863
Curran's motion once more and show the people living in Guelph

01:12:11.919 --> 01:12:14.995
today that you care. Thank you.

01:12:16.895 --> 01:12:21.167
Thank you so much. Is there any questions as a follow up hearing

01:12:21.191 --> 01:12:24.191
and seeing? None. Thank you very much. Thank you.

01:12:24.343 --> 01:12:27.983
Okay, Murray Costello and I

01:12:27.999 --> 01:12:31.551
think Patrick McMahon

01:12:31.623 --> 01:12:34.355
is maybe coming down together. Yes,

01:12:38.625 --> 01:12:42.565
thank you both for coming. And again, no more than five minutes.

01:12:42.905 --> 01:12:45.569
If you can share the five minutes if you want,

01:12:45.617 --> 01:12:49.737
or whatever you wish, but whatever it is, it's no more than five minutes and

01:12:49.761 --> 01:12:53.049
I'll turn it over to you whenever you're ready. Go right ahead. Okay, thank you.

01:12:53.217 --> 01:12:56.457
Good evening, your worship, Mayor Guthrie, council and city staff, thank you

01:12:56.481 --> 01:12:59.649
for the opportunity to share some information on Enbridge.

01:12:59.777 --> 01:13:03.153
More specifically the model franchise agreement that we're requesting, well,

01:13:03.289 --> 01:13:06.897
approval for. My name is Murray Costello. I'm the director of Southeast region

01:13:06.961 --> 01:13:10.273
operations. I have accountabilities for the maintenance, construction, operation of

01:13:10.289 --> 01:13:14.121
our natural gas system here in this area. Joining me this

01:13:14.153 --> 01:13:18.729
Evening is Patrick McMahon, our technical manager, regulatory affairs,

01:13:18.897 --> 01:13:22.889
taking a look at Enbridge. Here in Ontario we provide dependable energy to 3.9

01:13:22.937 --> 01:13:26.985
million customers. We are innovating and adapting our business model to accelerate

01:13:27.025 --> 01:13:29.835
the transition to a net zero emission future,

01:13:29.995 --> 01:13:33.939
focusing our efforts in three areas. Using less through conservation

01:13:34.027 --> 01:13:37.251
so programs for residential business, industrial customers.

01:13:37.443 --> 01:13:40.603
Transitioning to renewable gases. So think of renewable natural gas.

01:13:40.659 --> 01:13:44.963
Think of hydrogen advancing clean technologies for transportation,

01:13:45.139 --> 01:13:48.427
building heat and industrial processes such as cng,

01:13:48.531 --> 01:13:52.251
rng, hybrid heating, geothermal and carbon capture.

01:13:52.443 --> 01:13:55.939
Corporately, if I look back to 2002, Enbridge was an early

01:13:55.987 --> 01:13:59.299
adopter investing in renewable energy in both wind

01:13:59.347 --> 01:14:02.969
and solar. Since 2002, we've invested $10

01:14:03.017 --> 01:14:06.681
billion across North America and offshore.

01:14:06.793 --> 01:14:10.617
Currently we have wind farms in Concord and Dorian Richtown and solar farms in

01:14:10.641 --> 01:14:14.073
Sarnia, Tilbury and Amherstburg. When I take

01:14:14.089 --> 01:14:18.025
a look at the city of Guelph here, We've been serving natural

01:14:18.065 --> 01:14:20.765
gas to the city of Guelph since 1955.

01:14:21.585 --> 01:14:25.425
We are very proud to serve in industrial commercial residential customers

01:14:25.465 --> 01:14:28.517
of 48,000. We're a proud community partner

01:14:28.581 --> 01:14:32.293
pleased to have supported a number of initiatives in 2024 such as Wyndham

01:14:32.349 --> 01:14:35.705
House Children's Foundation, Guelph and Wellington School,

01:14:37.525 --> 01:14:41.149
Hopewell Children's Home Incorporated and Lakeside Hope

01:14:41.197 --> 01:14:44.765
House. I know that it came up the concept about relocation

01:14:44.845 --> 01:14:47.933
costs and I did go back and take a look over the last 10 years

01:14:48.029 --> 01:14:51.877
and there has been $11 million that Enbridge has invested in

01:14:51.901 --> 01:14:55.487
relocation programs here within the city of Guelph. Not one

01:14:55.511 --> 01:14:59.511
of those projects in the past 10 years required a contribution from

01:14:59.543 --> 01:15:03.095
the city of Guelph. So I know that there is talk about allocating and

01:15:03.175 --> 01:15:06.967
that can happen for pipe, that's post 1981.

01:15:06.991 --> 01:15:10.143
But I would look over the last 10 years, there's not the one project in

01:15:10.159 --> 01:15:13.511
the city of Guelph. Something I am proud about is the

01:15:13.583 --> 01:15:17.687
downtown Guelph revitalization project and working very closely with staff

01:15:17.751 --> 01:15:21.583
to work through that. So we did invest or we are investing

01:15:21.639 --> 01:15:25.935
this year $2.6 million this year to upgrade

01:15:25.975 --> 01:15:28.575
our system to move it out of the way to get out of the way

01:15:28.615 --> 01:15:31.655
for utility conflicts. Yes, there is an upside to our company

01:15:31.695 --> 01:15:35.543
is that we do have an upgraded system going into 2025.

01:15:35.599 --> 01:15:38.983
We'll invest another $2.5 million, no contribution

01:15:39.039 --> 01:15:42.503
from the city to update that infrastructure in

01:15:42.519 --> 01:15:45.795
the downtown, support the downtime revitalization.

01:15:46.615 --> 01:15:49.959
I know there's been the concept of subsidization come up and I

01:15:49.967 --> 01:15:53.875
did want to talk about property taxes. So across Ontario we do pay.

01:15:54.275 --> 01:15:57.347
In 2023 there was $124 million paid

01:15:57.411 --> 01:16:00.835
in property taxes. In Guelph, it would be slightly over

01:16:00.875 --> 01:16:04.427
$1 million that we did contribute to the city of Guelph.

01:16:04.451 --> 01:16:07.139
So we do pay taxes for every meter of pipe that we do have in

01:16:07.147 --> 01:16:10.827
the ground. On the municipal franchise agreement,

01:16:10.891 --> 01:16:14.875
it's been in place since January 2000. If we look at how it was started,

01:16:14.915 --> 01:16:16.415
it was between amo,

01:16:17.915 --> 01:16:21.095
the Ontario Energy Board and the gas utilities at that time.

01:16:21.795 --> 01:16:25.535
This current agreement does expire if I go to 5-16-20.

01:16:26.495 --> 01:16:29.783
The model franchise agreement was put in place to create a

01:16:29.799 --> 01:16:33.503
level playing field across Ontario. So we did not have one community

01:16:33.639 --> 01:16:37.383
subsidizing another community. It was an efficient model

01:16:37.439 --> 01:16:40.775
agreement created to create that equality

01:16:40.935 --> 01:16:43.991
across the province. The OAB does direct

01:16:44.023 --> 01:16:47.239
the franchise agreement to be based on the small agreement unless there's

01:16:47.287 --> 01:16:50.591
compelling reasons to deviate from it.

01:16:50.743 --> 01:16:54.303
When I look across Ontario Today, there is 342 of those agreements.

01:16:54.359 --> 01:16:58.427
I think there's only one minor deviation due to some certain

01:16:58.571 --> 01:17:01.655
geographic changes.

01:17:02.075 --> 01:17:04.923
When we look at the model agreement, yes, it's a 20 years agreement. There is

01:17:04.939 --> 01:17:08.019
a clause in there. So if changes do happen, they're approved by the OAB.

01:17:08.107 --> 01:17:12.395
They would be incorporated in on the seventh anniversary and the 14th anniversary

01:17:12.515 --> 01:17:16.323
of that agreement. Signing the franchise agreement doesn't force

01:17:16.379 --> 01:17:19.715
people to use natural gas.

01:17:19.875 --> 01:17:23.655
It creates that framework under which we would operate.

01:17:25.265 --> 01:17:28.561
So in closing, let me say we're committed to lowering emissions

01:17:28.593 --> 01:17:32.217
while meeting the energy demands of our customers. We aim to keep

01:17:32.321 --> 01:17:36.185
energy costs in check and anticipate our customers energy needs for tomorrow

01:17:36.305 --> 01:17:40.561
and develop new energy solutions as the energy evolution continues

01:17:40.593 --> 01:17:43.769
to unfold. We absolutely do need to maintain

01:17:43.817 --> 01:17:47.033
the reliability and the safety of the distribution network that

01:17:47.049 --> 01:17:50.943
we have in place today. Hence why we are requesting the renewal of

01:17:50.959 --> 01:17:53.715
the current model franchise agreement here in Guelph.

01:17:55.695 --> 01:18:00.035
Great, thank you very much. Is there any questions for this delegate?

01:18:00.375 --> 01:18:03.783
Councillor Karan, please. Great. Thank you, Mr. Mayor,

01:18:03.839 --> 01:18:07.511
through you and thanks for presenting this evening. You did mention that

01:18:07.543 --> 01:18:11.439
the history of the, of the model franchise agreement

01:18:11.487 --> 01:18:15.119
goes back 20 years. Yes, but you also acknowledge

01:18:15.167 --> 01:18:19.335
that the energy landscape has completely changed in the last 20 years.

01:18:19.675 --> 01:18:23.251
So would you agree, you know, without going into details

01:18:23.283 --> 01:18:26.995
about how a model franchise agreement could be changed, but wouldn't you agree

01:18:27.035 --> 01:18:30.219
that in a. Wouldn't you agree that a model

01:18:30.267 --> 01:18:34.299
franchise agreement that was developed 20 years ago when

01:18:34.387 --> 01:18:38.139
there's been a complete evolution of energy systems across Canada

01:18:38.307 --> 01:18:42.015
might be up for a little bit of work

01:18:42.525 --> 01:18:46.597
and change and updating, given that the energy landscape

01:18:46.661 --> 01:18:49.909
includes an entire renewable sector

01:18:49.997 --> 01:18:53.453
and transformation and the costs to

01:18:53.509 --> 01:18:56.825
municipalities have increased over time as well,

01:18:57.485 --> 01:19:00.093
Tiana, sure. Through you, Mr. Mayor,

01:19:00.269 --> 01:19:04.165
Pat McMahon here. The model franchise agreement actually

01:19:04.205 --> 01:19:08.021
originated back in 1987 and it was updated

01:19:08.133 --> 01:19:12.109
through request, through email that wanted to make updates to the model

01:19:12.157 --> 01:19:16.165
franchise agreement. So it's basically terms and conditions

01:19:16.785 --> 01:19:20.085
related to our operations within the municipality.

01:19:20.385 --> 01:19:24.233
It doesn't necessarily account for changes in energy

01:19:24.329 --> 01:19:28.161
frameworks or technology that

01:19:28.193 --> 01:19:31.925
might occur. It was actually written on purpose generically

01:19:32.225 --> 01:19:35.633
to address how we should operate within municipalities and

01:19:35.649 --> 01:19:39.811
leave it up to discussions with the municipalities on specifics on

01:19:39.953 --> 01:19:43.751
road work and follow up with staff at municipalities.

01:19:43.823 --> 01:19:47.555
So it doesn't actually contain a lot about framework

01:19:47.975 --> 01:19:51.695
or any changes in the energy regime

01:19:51.775 --> 01:19:55.415
in the, in the province. And the Ontario Energy

01:19:55.455 --> 01:19:59.663
Board hasn't been persuaded to change the model

01:19:59.719 --> 01:20:03.103
franchise agreement, mainly because they haven't had a lot

01:20:03.119 --> 01:20:07.073
of complaints about the model franchise agreement from municipalities or amor.

01:20:07.239 --> 01:20:11.517
It was a negotiated document led

01:20:11.581 --> 01:20:15.269
primarily by AMO in the day and with the gas utilities.

01:20:15.317 --> 01:20:18.205
So it was kind of negotiation at the time.

01:20:18.365 --> 01:20:21.877
And that's while the changes,

01:20:21.941 --> 01:20:25.349
some municipalities have suggested changes to the model franchise

01:20:25.397 --> 01:20:28.773
agreement, most of it is different

01:20:28.829 --> 01:20:32.157
conditions or refinements to reporting to

01:20:32.181 --> 01:20:36.075
the municipalities or municipal franchise

01:20:36.115 --> 01:20:39.643
fees and things like that. And the Interior Energy Board just

01:20:39.699 --> 01:20:42.763
has not agreed with making changes to

01:20:42.779 --> 01:20:46.395
the model franchise agreement because of the impact across the

01:20:46.435 --> 01:20:50.419
province if you try to make one off changes for one

01:20:50.467 --> 01:20:54.019
municipality when 300 plus other municipalities

01:20:54.107 --> 01:20:55.815
would be impacted as well.

01:20:59.645 --> 01:21:03.385
Thank you. And is there any other questions at this time?

01:21:03.965 --> 01:21:07.105
Okay, I have one. I'm just, I'm curious.

01:21:07.485 --> 01:21:10.725
So I. We often are hearing lately, especially from

01:21:10.765 --> 01:21:14.797
those that are sort of advocating for kind of some of these positions, that other

01:21:14.861 --> 01:21:18.309
provinces have completely different sort of setups

01:21:18.437 --> 01:21:21.605
that the utility, similar utility

01:21:21.645 --> 01:21:24.797
in a different province would be paying, you know, money to the

01:21:24.821 --> 01:21:28.189
municipality for the right of use of their roadways or,

01:21:28.317 --> 01:21:32.265
or you know, other agreements. Whereas in Ontario it's different.

01:21:32.565 --> 01:21:36.253
Do you, can you reflect on that for us? Like,

01:21:36.389 --> 01:21:39.853
why is it, do you think that Ontario is different than many of the other

01:21:39.909 --> 01:21:44.029
provinces where they, they have a different setup? I'm just curious if you

01:21:44.157 --> 01:21:46.825
have researched that. Why, why is it different?

01:21:47.565 --> 01:21:51.277
Well, thank you, Mr. Mayor. The other regimes.

01:21:51.381 --> 01:21:54.615
We'll talk for instance to Alberta where they do

01:21:54.655 --> 01:21:58.591
allow franchise fees to be collected through ATCO

01:21:58.663 --> 01:22:02.235
Gas. The difficulty they have there is the

01:22:02.815 --> 01:22:06.623
rate driver impact of these fees and the

01:22:06.679 --> 01:22:10.047
municipal franchise fees. In Alberta we're talking

01:22:10.111 --> 01:22:13.319
hundreds of millions. For instance,

01:22:13.487 --> 01:22:16.515
the city of Edmonton probably gets 70 million.

01:22:17.055 --> 01:22:20.343
Calgary gets maybe 80 million from ATCO Gas on

01:22:20.359 --> 01:22:23.805
an annual basis. So those costs are

01:22:23.845 --> 01:22:27.069
actually recovered from the rate pairs of Atco Gas.

01:22:27.197 --> 01:22:31.109
So. Right. So the rates will go up to recover those costs.

01:22:31.197 --> 01:22:35.077
And what they've determined is that instead of municipalities

01:22:35.181 --> 01:22:39.237
collecting revenue to pay for services through property

01:22:39.301 --> 01:22:43.493
taxes and other assessments, they're collecting it through Atco Gas.

01:22:43.629 --> 01:22:47.373
And so it's the same taxpayer acting as a gas

01:22:47.429 --> 01:22:49.585
ratepayer that's actually paying those fees.

01:22:50.465 --> 01:22:53.721
Okay. Thank you. All right. Oh,

01:22:53.753 --> 01:22:56.805
I see. I see a hand. I see a hand. Counselor Billings.

01:22:58.665 --> 01:23:02.009
So through your worship. Okay. Given what you just said,

01:23:02.097 --> 01:23:05.545
you're aware of the recommendation in front of us and

01:23:05.585 --> 01:23:09.257
council would like to see you being charged fees for the

01:23:09.281 --> 01:23:11.565
use of public property. However,

01:23:12.105 --> 01:23:15.747
clause 3C would like to

01:23:15.771 --> 01:23:19.131
see an agreement which will ensure future charges for

01:23:19.163 --> 01:23:22.507
use of municipal property is not passed on

01:23:22.531 --> 01:23:26.895
to the Guelph customers of the gas distribution company.

01:23:27.555 --> 01:23:31.075
So when I read that, I think it sounds politically good

01:23:31.115 --> 01:23:34.859
but not realistic. So I wanted you to comment on that. And given what you

01:23:34.907 --> 01:23:38.811
just said, is it not the likelihood that

01:23:38.923 --> 01:23:42.955
these fees would be passed on to the ratepayers

01:23:44.735 --> 01:23:46.155
through you? Mr. Mayor?

01:23:47.895 --> 01:23:51.407
The proposed legislation is to try to make

01:23:51.471 --> 01:23:54.895
changes to the regulation that doesn't allow these

01:23:54.935 --> 01:23:58.755
fees to be charged to utilities like Enbridge Gas.

01:24:00.895 --> 01:24:04.711
And to get the municipal franchise agreement changed,

01:24:04.743 --> 01:24:08.863
we would have to make an application to the Ontario Energy Board to accept

01:24:09.039 --> 01:24:13.915
clauses addressing this proposed legislation.

01:24:14.495 --> 01:24:18.663
And from our experience, the Interior Energy Board would not even recognize

01:24:18.799 --> 01:24:22.675
speculative legislation. So they wouldn't

01:24:22.975 --> 01:24:26.207
act on speculative legislation.

01:24:26.271 --> 01:24:29.415
They act on legislation or directions

01:24:29.455 --> 01:24:33.375
from the minister, but anything speculative they do not act

01:24:33.415 --> 01:24:36.773
on. So they wouldn't do anything

01:24:36.829 --> 01:24:40.405
with a recommendation to add clauses talking

01:24:40.445 --> 01:24:43.693
about proposed legislation as far as

01:24:43.789 --> 01:24:47.613
recovery of municipal franchise

01:24:47.669 --> 01:24:51.765
fees if they were ever passed as

01:24:51.885 --> 01:24:55.701
accepted. Ontario Energy Board has a practice, as Mr.

01:24:55.733 --> 01:24:59.373
Costello said earlier, to approve the recovery

01:24:59.429 --> 01:25:03.111
of permit fees or other costs that are incurred by

01:25:03.143 --> 01:25:06.623
Enbridge Gas to provide service through rates.

01:25:06.799 --> 01:25:10.647
So they would be. Ontario Energy Board

01:25:10.831 --> 01:25:14.751
in all likelihood would allow recovery of prudently incurred

01:25:14.863 --> 01:25:18.175
franchise fees through the rates charged to the ratepayers

01:25:18.215 --> 01:25:22.447
and the citizens of the city of Guelph. So it's

01:25:22.631 --> 01:25:26.711
not something we would not recommend to change the

01:25:26.783 --> 01:25:30.195
board's long standing policy of recovering costs and rates

01:25:30.545 --> 01:25:34.121
because the benefit rates follow the

01:25:34.153 --> 01:25:37.473
benefits. So if the taxpayers of the city of

01:25:37.489 --> 01:25:40.649
Guelph are benefiting from a franchise fee,

01:25:40.777 --> 01:25:44.337
that's who should be paying for the benefit. And so that's the

01:25:44.361 --> 01:25:47.785
longstanding policy of the Ontario Energy Board. And that's what we would

01:25:47.825 --> 01:25:51.245
continue to pursue. Thank you for that.

01:25:52.945 --> 01:25:55.765
Thanks. Councillor Billings. Over to Councilor Ault. Please.

01:25:56.105 --> 01:25:59.565
Thank you very much. Mayor Guthrie. Through you to the delegates.

01:26:00.055 --> 01:26:03.935
I apologize. I don't know why my thing is dinging. I got it on silent.

01:26:04.095 --> 01:26:07.527
That was my wife. The question that

01:26:07.551 --> 01:26:11.263
I have for you didn't come up until I heard a couple of the

01:26:11.279 --> 01:26:15.435
other councillors ask questions. I'm struggling with an issue of transparency and equity.

01:26:16.375 --> 01:26:20.055
One of the things that strikes me about this that's good, whether the

01:26:20.135 --> 01:26:23.151
costs are passed on to the consumers or not,

01:26:23.263 --> 01:26:26.775
is that there's a very clear transparency, that this is part of the

01:26:26.815 --> 01:26:29.095
cost of using natural gas,

01:26:30.195 --> 01:26:33.691
which I think is important. I do believe in transparency. The other one

01:26:33.723 --> 01:26:37.139
is equity. It strikes me as well that

01:26:37.227 --> 01:26:40.491
consumers and residents of Guelph or any other community

01:26:40.603 --> 01:26:44.603
who are not using gas, but are perhaps using electricity

01:26:44.739 --> 01:26:48.175
or maybe in the odd case, completely off the grid,

01:26:48.595 --> 01:26:52.615
are actually being charged through our taxes

01:26:53.475 --> 01:26:57.765
inadvertently for Enbridge, essentially using

