01:41:26.673 --> 01:41:29.889
to get cut off. Okay, thank you so much. Well,

01:41:29.937 --> 01:41:33.617
good evening, mayor, council members, staff and residents. And thank you for allowing

01:41:33.641 --> 01:41:36.873
me the time just to speak on your

01:41:36.969 --> 01:41:40.473
busy agenda. My name is Gabby Kalapush and I'm the Clean Air. I work

01:41:40.489 --> 01:41:43.953
at the Clean Air Partnership and wanted to speak to Clean Air Partnership support for

01:41:43.969 --> 01:41:47.329
the city of Guelph hopeful leadership in addressing an unfair

01:41:47.377 --> 01:41:50.741
fossil fuel subsidy forced upon municipalities in Ontario.

01:41:50.913 --> 01:41:54.485
Clean Air Partnership is a charitable environmental organization whose modus

01:41:54.525 --> 01:41:57.925
operandi is to work with municipalities and their partners to make

01:41:57.965 --> 01:42:01.701
progress on the significant gap between climate commitments and climate

01:42:01.773 --> 01:42:05.501
action implementation in one of many examples

01:42:05.573 --> 01:42:09.237
of how life is not fair, it is the truth that municipal budgets

01:42:09.261 --> 01:42:12.901
are extremely vulnerable to significant costs from climate change,

01:42:13.053 --> 01:42:16.765
especially as it relates to the risk of infrastructure or residents resulting

01:42:16.805 --> 01:42:20.909
from extreme weather. Municipal budgets are also extremely limited

01:42:20.957 --> 01:42:24.565
in their revenue sources, with property taxes being their only or main

01:42:24.605 --> 01:42:28.117
recourse for revenue generation. This is a significant

01:42:28.261 --> 01:42:31.093
challenge for municipal budgets, as I'm sure you all know,

01:42:31.229 --> 01:42:35.021
and how we are how are we going to secure the needed resources to

01:42:35.053 --> 01:42:38.437
invest in the climate commitments and respond to the costs related to the

01:42:38.461 --> 01:42:42.317
impacts of extreme weather and climate change? It's a question that

01:42:42.341 --> 01:42:45.767
we're all asking ourselves. It is also true that over

01:42:45.831 --> 01:42:49.855
95% of Guelph's greenhouse gas emissions comes from the use of fossil fuels.

01:42:49.975 --> 01:42:53.743
I get that the franchise agreement is not an exciting thing to dig into,

01:42:53.879 --> 01:42:57.447
but how utilities use the municipal right of way and shining light on

01:42:57.471 --> 01:43:01.375
the very unequal playing field between how Ontario's fossil

01:43:01.415 --> 01:43:05.407
fuel system is paid for, how low carbon energy systems are

01:43:05.591 --> 01:43:09.343
paid for and how our electricity is paid for is one of the key

01:43:09.399 --> 01:43:13.041
critical things we need to address to create a level playing field

01:43:13.113 --> 01:43:17.329
on energy choices for ontarians. It continually

01:43:17.417 --> 01:43:21.289
amazes me that we make low carbon energy systems compete on their own

01:43:21.337 --> 01:43:25.569
against a fossil fuel system that receives significant fossil fuel subsidies.

01:43:25.737 --> 01:43:28.777
Be these fossil fuel subsidies from taxation relief

01:43:28.881 --> 01:43:32.409
or subsidizing all infrastructure costs off the

01:43:32.497 --> 01:43:35.745
provincial fossil fuel rate base, or not having to pay their

01:43:35.785 --> 01:43:39.155
carbon price for electricity generated via fossil fuels.

01:43:39.485 --> 01:43:42.877
All these are examples of fossil fuel subsidies that Enbridge,

01:43:42.941 --> 01:43:45.625
which is a private company, benefits from.

01:43:46.285 --> 01:43:49.741
Provincial rules now allowing municipalities to charge Enbridge

01:43:49.773 --> 01:43:53.365
for use of the right of way for pipelines is an example of a fossil

01:43:53.405 --> 01:43:56.373
fuel subsidy that is forced upon municipalities.

01:43:56.549 --> 01:44:00.533
Municipalities need to draw attention to these issues to get this on the provincial

01:44:00.589 --> 01:44:04.105
agenda. The province certainly won't address these issues without

01:44:04.445 --> 01:44:07.813
municipalities asking them to. Municipalities also

01:44:07.869 --> 01:44:12.185
need to address their limited revenue tools for their climate commitments implementation.

01:44:12.565 --> 01:44:15.997
Municipalities in other provinces can charge utilities for use of the right of

01:44:16.021 --> 01:44:19.253
way for their infrastructure, so why not Ontario municipalities?

01:44:19.389 --> 01:44:23.485
In addition, being able to charge Enbridge will help level the playing field between fossil

01:44:23.525 --> 01:44:27.029
fuel and low carbon energy utilities. The fossil fuel

01:44:27.077 --> 01:44:30.173
subsidy forced upon Ontario municipalities doesn't end at the free use

01:44:30.189 --> 01:44:34.131
of the municipal right of way mutual Enbridge also has the given given

01:44:34.203 --> 01:44:37.947
has been given the authority to abandon its infrastructure in the municipal right away.

01:44:38.051 --> 01:44:41.219
And while this may not have reared its head as yet a problem,

01:44:41.387 --> 01:44:44.483
but as we move away from fossil fuels in the Energy transition,

01:44:44.619 --> 01:44:48.347
this will start to become a municipal issue. Why should they have the right to

01:44:48.371 --> 01:44:51.723
abandon their infrastructure and leave it to municipalities to deal with?

01:44:51.779 --> 01:44:55.115
I'm very confused by that. Municipal leadership

01:44:55.155 --> 01:44:58.523
in calling out these unfair practices being forced upon municipalities

01:44:58.579 --> 01:45:01.605
is critical to driving provincial engagement on this topic.

01:45:01.755 --> 01:45:05.553
Change won't happen without municipal leadership. And unless municipalities

01:45:05.609 --> 01:45:08.865
like Guelph care a whole awful lot, Ontario's energy system

01:45:08.905 --> 01:45:12.305
is not going to ensure a more level playing field between our fossil fuel system

01:45:12.345 --> 01:45:15.617
of the past and the low carbon energy systems of

01:45:15.641 --> 01:45:19.353
the future and present. Ontario Canada needs to be a participant in

01:45:19.369 --> 01:45:22.745
that low energy transformation and removing fossil fuel subsidies

01:45:22.785 --> 01:45:26.361
such as free access to the right of way and the right to abandon infrastructure

01:45:26.393 --> 01:45:30.345
as part of that needed change. These subsidies are not fair. It's really

01:45:30.385 --> 01:45:33.665
not. Thank you so much for your time and I'd be loved to answer

01:45:33.705 --> 01:45:35.285
any questions that you may have.

01:45:37.385 --> 01:45:40.489
Thank you Gabby for your presentation. We appreciate that.

01:45:40.617 --> 01:45:43.913
And let me see if there's any follow up for you. Councillor Alt has a

01:45:43.929 --> 01:45:47.521
question for you. Thank you Mayor Guthrie. I now see where I got my confused

01:45:47.593 --> 01:45:50.881
understanding through you.

01:45:50.913 --> 01:45:55.473
To the delegate, I was wondering if you could outline what is Bill 160 and

01:45:55.529 --> 01:45:59.057
what is its apparent impact on new

01:45:59.161 --> 01:46:02.313
housing development costs. Thank you so

01:46:02.329 --> 01:46:05.849
much. And through the chair. Bill 165 was a

01:46:05.897 --> 01:46:09.881
bill passed by the Province of Ontario after the Ontario Energy Board

01:46:09.953 --> 01:46:13.985
ruled that Enbridge should not be making the rate base subsidize the

01:46:14.025 --> 01:46:18.001
cost of new infrastructure in particular to low rise development.

01:46:18.153 --> 01:46:21.857
So this is really geared towards how the fossil fuel infrastructure

01:46:21.881 --> 01:46:25.543
cost costs are paid for to new developments. And what

01:46:25.599 --> 01:46:29.287
the Ontario Energy Board said is that it's not fair for the Ontario

01:46:29.351 --> 01:46:33.287
rate base to subsidize these infrastructure costs in this

01:46:33.351 --> 01:46:37.159
it should be there, should be the cost being put

01:46:37.207 --> 01:46:40.647
on to development should pay for the cost of development. It's very

01:46:40.671 --> 01:46:44.327
much similar along the lines of most development charges in that approach

01:46:44.431 --> 01:46:48.263
that it should infrastructure costs should be allocated to those

01:46:48.359 --> 01:46:51.643
that are in create the infrastructure cost in the first place and it

01:46:51.659 --> 01:46:54.843
should be added to the development costs as a whole and that the

01:46:54.859 --> 01:46:58.283
rate they should not subsidize that. So what ended up happening is the

01:46:58.299 --> 01:47:01.739
Province of Ontario passed Bill 165 to maintain

01:47:01.827 --> 01:47:05.627
that fossil fuel subsidy in new developments. And right now we

01:47:05.651 --> 01:47:10.211
have it where you know, we have to pay for electricity allocations,

01:47:10.403 --> 01:47:13.995
but we don't have to pay for fossil fuel infrastructure costs being brought to new

01:47:14.035 --> 01:47:17.659
development. It creates an unequal playing field between electricity, how electricity

01:47:17.707 --> 01:47:21.155
system is costed out and how the fossil fuel system is costed out.

01:47:23.255 --> 01:47:27.543
One last question, Mayor Kathryn so then my original understanding

01:47:27.679 --> 01:47:31.575
that non gas consumers are actually

01:47:31.735 --> 01:47:35.375
subsidizing gas lines into subdivisions,

01:47:35.455 --> 01:47:36.595
is that correct?

01:47:38.495 --> 01:47:42.311
They non gas users would not be subsidizing the

01:47:42.343 --> 01:47:45.687
cost of new infrastructure, new fossil fuel infrastructure for new developments,

01:47:45.711 --> 01:47:49.019
because they're not an Enbridge customer. But all Enbridge

01:47:49.067 --> 01:47:52.411
customers are subsidizing the costs associated with bringing

01:47:52.483 --> 01:47:56.043
the fossil fuel infrastructure to new developments. One of

01:47:56.059 --> 01:47:59.491
the big concerns that the Ontario Energy Board kind of raises is the risk

01:47:59.523 --> 01:48:03.083
of stranded assets. And one of the key kind of issues for

01:48:03.179 --> 01:48:07.091
why this does increase the risk of stranded assets is

01:48:07.123 --> 01:48:10.643
because, for example, it takes approximately 40 years.

01:48:10.699 --> 01:48:14.401
Let's use that as an example for the the

01:48:14.433 --> 01:48:18.097
rate base user, the customer, to pay back the rate

01:48:18.161 --> 01:48:21.425
base for that infrastructure investment. However,

01:48:21.505 --> 01:48:25.129
if you're bringing new development, new infrastructure to a new development

01:48:25.297 --> 01:48:28.081
and they're on the fossil fuel, we know that they're going to be on fossil

01:48:28.113 --> 01:48:32.041
fuels for at least 15 years while their fossil fuel furnace is

01:48:32.073 --> 01:48:36.017
functioning. However, when their next kind of, when that fossil fuel furnace comes

01:48:36.041 --> 01:48:39.193
to its end of life, there is a good likelihood that that

01:48:39.249 --> 01:48:42.937
customer will actually move over to a heat pump and then that way

01:48:43.001 --> 01:48:46.393
move or move themselves for the Enbridge as an Enbridge customer,

01:48:46.489 --> 01:48:50.369
in which case the overall rate base would be the one who has to subsidize

01:48:50.417 --> 01:48:53.913
that infrastructure cost. Enbridge is guaranteed, I think it's a

01:48:53.929 --> 01:48:56.889
9% rate of return on all of their investments.

01:48:56.977 --> 01:49:01.009
And then they make additional profits by selling fossil fuels to

01:49:01.057 --> 01:49:05.329
Enbridge Ontario's Enbridge system. So there's the Enbridge

01:49:05.377 --> 01:49:08.697
Canadian company, but then there's the Enbridge Ontario company.

01:49:08.881 --> 01:49:12.305
Enbridge Canada Company makes its money off the sale of fossil

01:49:12.345 --> 01:49:16.225
fuels and Enbridge Ontario company makes its money off

01:49:16.265 --> 01:49:19.485
the bringing, paying, getting delivery, getting,

01:49:19.865 --> 01:49:23.745
recouping the cost of the infrastructure. And if we have the risk

01:49:23.785 --> 01:49:27.521
of stranded assets, it increases the risk that those who can't leave the

01:49:27.553 --> 01:49:31.313
fossil fuel system are going to be creating are going

01:49:31.329 --> 01:49:35.345
to be unfairly burdened with stranded asset costs that

01:49:35.385 --> 01:49:38.817
have to be recouped because Enbridge is guaranteed its 9% rate

01:49:38.841 --> 01:49:41.951
of return. Thank you.

01:49:42.063 --> 01:49:43.555
I've learned something today.

01:49:46.455 --> 01:49:50.127
Oh, Councillor Karan, please. Thank you. And thank you for an

01:49:50.151 --> 01:49:53.675
excellent presentation. And one of the things that you highlighted

01:49:54.215 --> 01:49:57.831
that was significant for me is that change is coming.

01:49:57.903 --> 01:50:01.375
It's the end of the carbon economy. We're moving to a low carbon

01:50:01.415 --> 01:50:04.911
economy or no carbon economy and that the transition to heat pumps needs to be

01:50:04.943 --> 01:50:09.055
incentivized and creating the Level playing field is

01:50:09.135 --> 01:50:13.063
a way to make that happen. We heard in the presentation earlier, and I

01:50:13.079 --> 01:50:16.367
don't know if you were listening online, but we heard in the earlier presentation from

01:50:16.391 --> 01:50:19.551
Enbridge that they are investing

01:50:19.703 --> 01:50:23.239
millions into conservation and

01:50:23.407 --> 01:50:27.463
new technologies. And it was my understanding that

01:50:27.519 --> 01:50:31.359
the Canada Greener Homes initiative is actually the delivery.

01:50:31.447 --> 01:50:35.465
Enbridge is only the delivery agent for a federal program.

01:50:36.085 --> 01:50:40.085
So the reason it is relevant here is if we want to incentivize

01:50:40.205 --> 01:50:44.061
by having the OEB change the

01:50:44.253 --> 01:50:48.629
way we municipalities can gain revenue and incentivize

01:50:48.717 --> 01:50:52.509
low energy economy, we have to acknowledge

01:50:52.557 --> 01:50:56.021
that these grants aren't

01:50:56.173 --> 01:51:00.231
being delivered by our fossil gas suppliers. They're coming from

01:51:00.303 --> 01:51:03.919
public tax dollars. Can you shed some light on

01:51:04.087 --> 01:51:08.503
that structure? Yeah, I should actually mention that Enbridge

01:51:08.559 --> 01:51:12.055
actually was delivering the Greener Homes. I will give Enbridge credit.

01:51:12.135 --> 01:51:15.543
They did a better job of delivering the Greener homes from a customer

01:51:15.599 --> 01:51:19.287
perspective than Greener Homes was doing. So I will give them credit. They streamlined

01:51:19.311 --> 01:51:22.727
the process, they made it a better experience for

01:51:22.791 --> 01:51:25.927
people participating in the Greener Homes program. They were simply the

01:51:25.951 --> 01:51:29.253
administrators of it. I'm sure they made a certain amount of money as

01:51:29.349 --> 01:51:32.861
administering the program on behalf of Greener Homes,

01:51:32.973 --> 01:51:36.037
but I will give them a shout out for that. They did do improvement in

01:51:36.061 --> 01:51:39.397
terms of customer service delivery on that one. I will also say

01:51:39.421 --> 01:51:43.333
that's not Enbridge money, that is taxpayer money. We're all Canadian

01:51:43.389 --> 01:51:47.005
citizens, we all paying for that kind of. That's coming from all of us.

01:51:47.125 --> 01:51:50.725
I should also mention that their DSM money, their demand side management

01:51:50.805 --> 01:51:54.525
money is also not Enbridge money, it's ratepayer money. So all of

01:51:54.565 --> 01:51:57.885
these services, when we kind of talk about incentives, it's not Enbridge

01:51:57.925 --> 01:52:01.469
giving us that incentive. Yes they are the delivery for that incentive.

01:52:01.557 --> 01:52:05.077
But ultimately it's either the taxpayer or the ratepayer who's paying for

01:52:05.101 --> 01:52:08.605
it. So yes, we need more incentives. But yes,

01:52:08.645 --> 01:52:11.733
we also need to move away from incentives. We need to kind of creating a

01:52:11.749 --> 01:52:15.349
level playing field for people to have a more equal choice.

01:52:15.477 --> 01:52:19.709
Would I choose as a new customer if I had to pay $5,000

01:52:19.797 --> 01:52:23.597
for bringing fossil fuels or would I go all electric in my building if I

01:52:23.621 --> 01:52:27.297
didn't have kind of pay that kind of thing. But it's forced upon me as

01:52:27.321 --> 01:52:31.241
a, as a new homeowner, I'm guaranteed to get those pipelines brought

01:52:31.273 --> 01:52:34.657
into it. Why? Because it's all subsidized and the developer doesn't pay

01:52:34.681 --> 01:52:38.001
for it. So they want Enbridge to come in because they don't actually take

01:52:38.033 --> 01:52:40.849
on those costs and additional electricity allocation.

01:52:41.017 --> 01:52:44.513
So I did want to kind of highlight all of these things are all incentives

01:52:44.569 --> 01:52:48.209
that we as either taxpayers or ratepayers are paying for.

01:52:48.297 --> 01:52:51.457
They're not coming from the companies. And I should also mention one

01:52:51.481 --> 01:52:55.017
more thing on this one we have to move away from the incentives. I'm a

01:52:55.041 --> 01:52:59.137
fan of incentives but like let's face it, we all want incentives

01:52:59.241 --> 01:53:02.929
and nobody wants to pay for these incentives but they have to be paid for.

01:53:03.017 --> 01:53:06.617
So we really got to move towards financing and we

01:53:06.641 --> 01:53:10.009
have to move towards a level playing field because the incentives and

01:53:10.057 --> 01:53:13.401
scaling up of these actions that we all knew need

01:53:13.433 --> 01:53:16.993
to get scaled up, we can't afford to do it via incentive model.

01:53:17.089 --> 01:53:20.265
We have to do it by creating a level playing field in our energy system

01:53:20.305 --> 01:53:24.177
and providing real choices for consumers

01:53:24.241 --> 01:53:28.209
to choose from. But that choice

01:53:28.257 --> 01:53:30.805
will only be possible if we create a level playing field.

01:53:32.105 --> 01:53:32.965
Okay,

01:53:35.665 --> 01:53:38.913
so there are no more questions but I am still genuinely curious.

01:53:38.969 --> 01:53:42.489
So I'm going to be your final question. Gabby. Thank you.

01:53:42.577 --> 01:53:46.129
You mentioned the other provinces, how it's different.

01:53:46.257 --> 01:53:51.163
Fair enough, we all agree with that. But it

01:53:51.179 --> 01:53:54.975
is the rate payer then that will end up paying.

01:53:56.475 --> 01:53:59.875
Square that for me. Yeah, the rate payer

01:53:59.915 --> 01:54:03.019
will probably pay it because Ontario Energy Board will have to.

01:54:03.067 --> 01:54:07.091
There's only one pot of money that needs to come into it and Ontario,

01:54:07.203 --> 01:54:10.563
Ontario Energy Board like, like Enbridge is going to get its 9%.

01:54:10.659 --> 01:54:13.763
If Enbridge is truly worried about affordability, maybe they should take it

01:54:13.779 --> 01:54:17.219
off their profit margin. I'm just saying they never talk about, they always

01:54:17.267 --> 01:54:20.811
talk about affordability and adding rate bases but they never talk about

01:54:20.843 --> 01:54:24.187
it from kind of like oh let's do a little bit of rate based sharing

01:54:24.251 --> 01:54:27.347
in terms of like the, you know, that kind of mentioning about saving on the

01:54:27.371 --> 01:54:30.947
distribution system. I would kind be very curious myself to see

01:54:30.971 --> 01:54:35.283
what kind of sharing, profit sharing has taken place on that one between the ratepayers

01:54:35.419 --> 01:54:39.155
and Enbridge. But ultimately when it comes down to it, we're all paying

01:54:39.195 --> 01:54:42.487
for these choices whether we know it or not, whether we're

01:54:42.511 --> 01:54:45.615
paying for it at present or whether we're paying for it in the future.

01:54:45.735 --> 01:54:49.047
And I think one of the key things that this is, that's why the level

01:54:49.111 --> 01:54:52.775
playing field is so important is providing real

01:54:52.855 --> 01:54:56.183
choices to ontarians is the goal on this one.

01:54:56.359 --> 01:55:00.087
We know we do not have a level playing field. We know we give free

01:55:00.151 --> 01:55:03.399
infrastructure to new developments. We know that we don't

01:55:03.447 --> 01:55:07.327
charge franchise agreements and many of like other different provinces, we know

01:55:07.351 --> 01:55:10.771
that there's cost associated with. Because the reason this issue came

01:55:10.803 --> 01:55:14.155
to our attention at Clean Air Partnership is because we found that some

01:55:14.195 --> 01:55:17.723
municipalities were actually charging their low carbon utilities for

01:55:17.779 --> 01:55:20.899
use of the right of way. And when we asked, wait a sec, why are

01:55:20.907 --> 01:55:24.227
you charging your low carbon utilities for access to the right of way and

01:55:24.251 --> 01:55:28.267
why not Enbridge? They informed us that it's not their choice. They're not allowed to

01:55:28.291 --> 01:55:32.203
charge Enbridge, but they say that they have real charges and real costs

01:55:32.299 --> 01:55:35.691
associated with infrastructure in the right of way. It could be

01:55:35.723 --> 01:55:38.363
something as simple as like you can't go with a truck because it's like you

01:55:38.379 --> 01:55:41.661
gotta go in and with a hand person and dig it out. And we've been

01:55:41.693 --> 01:55:44.869
asking municipalities to try and kind of calculate those costs.

01:55:44.917 --> 01:55:48.405
Not easy to do, unfortunately. But ultimately what it comes

01:55:48.445 --> 01:55:51.901
down to is we have to find a more level playing field and

01:55:51.933 --> 01:55:56.133
create more transparency in our energy system for who pays for what and

01:55:56.189 --> 01:56:00.045
correct the subsidies that are in place in order to provide

01:56:00.125 --> 01:56:03.541
real choices to ontarians and reduce the

01:56:03.573 --> 01:56:07.583
cost associated with the rate base as a whole as well. Because. Because that's a

01:56:07.599 --> 01:56:10.903
different one. But there's another topic that we can go into. No problem.

01:56:11.079 --> 01:56:14.295
No, no, no problem. You did answer

01:56:14.335 --> 01:56:17.527
the question. So I do appreciate that. And with that, I'm just

01:56:17.551 --> 01:56:20.839
looking around one more time. There is no more questions. But Gabby, thank you so

01:56:20.847 --> 01:56:24.223
much for joining us and thank you for your presentation tonight. Okay, thank you very

01:56:24.239 --> 01:56:27.063
much for allowing me the time on your agenda. I really appreciate it. Have a

01:56:27.079 --> 01:56:30.623
good evening. Yes, you as well. Take care. Thank you.

01:56:30.759 --> 01:56:34.263
Okay, Gina, Gina Lamel is here

01:56:34.319 --> 01:56:38.141
from the Seniors for Climate Action. Now, front row, a Keener.

01:56:38.293 --> 01:56:41.765
Thank you for coming and waiting. And then, Evan, I saw you walk

01:56:41.805 --> 01:56:45.533
in. You're in the wings. Okay. You're going to close us off tonight. So Gina,

01:56:45.589 --> 01:56:48.573
thanks for waiting and I'll turn it over to you. Okay, Go ahead. Thank you.

01:56:48.629 --> 01:56:52.381
Mr. Mayor, members of council and city staff, good evening.

01:56:52.493 --> 01:56:56.037
To refresh your memory, I'm a lifelong resident of Guelph and I

01:56:56.061 --> 01:56:59.413
am the chair of the Guelph chapter of Seniors for Climate Action Now.

01:56:59.549 --> 01:57:02.879
We are the Canadian group of older adults acting to address

01:57:02.927 --> 01:57:06.595
the climate emergency and protect the planet for future generations.

01:57:07.415 --> 01:57:11.167
Last fall, while driving in my car with my 19

01:57:11.231 --> 01:57:14.903
year old son, we heard yet another news report of an

01:57:14.919 --> 01:57:18.087
out of control wildfire. I asked him what

01:57:18.111 --> 01:57:20.195
he thought about the climate crisis.

01:57:21.015 --> 01:57:24.799
He's a very optimistic, positive young man. But his

01:57:24.847 --> 01:57:28.657
reply was, we are doomed. He and many

01:57:28.721 --> 01:57:32.405
others, but especially youth like the youth who are here this evening

01:57:32.745 --> 01:57:36.721
believe that we are on a trajectory to an unsustainable planet.

01:57:36.793 --> 01:57:40.137
And so do I. This is why we

01:57:40.161 --> 01:57:44.345
at SCAN are very grateful for the first step you have taken against

01:57:44.425 --> 01:57:47.801
fossil fuel giant Enbridge. Today, we're asking

01:57:47.833 --> 01:57:51.817
you to take another bold step and approve all of the recommendations

01:57:51.921 --> 01:57:54.365
concerning the Enbridge franchise Agreement.

01:57:54.985 --> 01:57:58.925
Approval would be staying true to your publicly stated priorities.

01:57:59.465 --> 01:58:03.633
In 2022, Mayor Guthrie was appointed, as we've all heard,

01:58:03.769 --> 01:58:07.217
to the Board of Mayors. Sorry, Board of Directors of the Global

01:58:07.281 --> 01:58:10.761
Covenant of Mayors for Climate and Energy. And here

01:58:10.793 --> 01:58:14.209
is what that website says. In his

01:58:14.257 --> 01:58:18.169
role as a GCOM Board member, Mayor Guthrie will serve as an ambassador

01:58:18.217 --> 01:58:20.899
for urban climate action at local, local,

01:58:21.067 --> 01:58:24.627
national and global levels and represent the region

01:58:24.691 --> 01:58:27.915
of North America, helping guide North America

01:58:27.995 --> 01:58:31.395
towards ambitious emissions reduction goals and

01:58:31.435 --> 01:58:35.695
helping national governments meet the highest ambitions of the Paris Agreement.

01:58:36.275 --> 01:58:40.523
It also says the City of Guelph has implemented multiple initiatives

01:58:40.579 --> 01:58:44.243
to cut emissions, earning a reputation for environmental

01:58:44.299 --> 01:58:47.835
and energy leadership. In Canada, the City

01:58:47.875 --> 01:58:51.683
is replacing its diesel transit fleet with electric

01:58:51.739 --> 01:58:55.435
buses, developing the first circular food economy in

01:58:55.475 --> 01:58:59.299
Canada, banning single use plastics, and is committed

01:58:59.347 --> 01:59:02.015
to becoming a net Zero community.

01:59:03.195 --> 01:59:06.475
Mr. Mayor and Council, I challenge you to recommit

01:59:06.515 --> 01:59:10.571
yourself to these ambitious emissions reduction goals and

01:59:10.603 --> 01:59:14.437
climate initiatives. Be an example and guide

01:59:14.501 --> 01:59:18.021
communities in Ontario and across North America to do the

01:59:18.053 --> 01:59:21.637
same. Be the climate leaders that GCOM says that

01:59:21.661 --> 01:59:25.381
you are. The City's website also affirms that the

01:59:25.413 --> 01:59:29.365
City is committed to the United Nations Race to Zero and has set targets

01:59:29.405 --> 01:59:33.765
for the whole community to reduce carbon emissions by 63%

01:59:33.925 --> 01:59:37.823
and by 2030 and to work together to become a net

01:59:37.879 --> 01:59:40.395
zero carbon community by 2050.

01:59:41.455 --> 01:59:44.935
Proposing significant cuts to environmental investments as are

01:59:44.975 --> 01:59:49.195
proposed in the 2025 budget is not consistent with these commitments.

01:59:49.575 --> 01:59:53.079
Although the City continues to invest in transit and active

01:59:53.127 --> 01:59:56.415
transportation initiatives, the overall reduction in funding

01:59:56.455 --> 02:00:00.463
will slow down progress on electrification and other essential environmental

02:00:00.519 --> 02:00:04.119
projects without significant change like the change that

02:00:04.167 --> 02:00:08.179
that young man man spoke about. Guelph is on the trajectory to

02:00:08.227 --> 02:00:11.579
climate doom. Guelph's community greenhouse

02:00:11.627 --> 02:00:15.935
gas emissions are up 8% compared to 2021.

02:00:16.275 --> 02:00:19.947
Guelph needs climate leadership like that touted on the

02:00:19.971 --> 02:00:24.195
GCOM webpage. We need bold decisions, radical shifts,

02:00:24.235 --> 02:00:27.931
and we need every one of the environmental initiatives that you are planning to

02:00:27.963 --> 02:00:30.765
defer or reduce and we need them now.

02:00:31.215 --> 02:00:34.887
The GCOM website says that Guelph has a reputation

02:00:34.951 --> 02:00:39.519
for environmental and energy leadership, but that was in 2022.

02:00:39.687 --> 02:00:43.247
We want and need that kind of leadership again and we need that

02:00:43.311 --> 02:00:46.655
now. The United Nations Intergovernmental Panel

02:00:46.695 --> 02:00:50.903
on Climate Change states that there's a rapidly closing window of opportunity

02:00:50.959 --> 02:00:54.183
to secure a livable and sustainable future for all of

02:00:54.199 --> 02:00:57.999
us. And the choices and actions implemented in the next five years

02:00:58.047 --> 02:01:01.617
will have impacted now and will reverberate for hundreds,

02:01:01.681 --> 02:01:05.225
even thousands of years. COP28 concluded

02:01:05.265 --> 02:01:09.185
with an urgent message emphasizing this need for a radical shift.

02:01:09.345 --> 02:01:12.825
COP 29 just ended and is calling

02:01:12.865 --> 02:01:15.645
for us to bolster climate action.

02:01:16.505 --> 02:01:20.217
Climate action requires public financing, no doubt, but there's

02:01:20.241 --> 02:01:24.609
at least one way to minimize the burden on the public purse and that's renegotiate

02:01:24.657 --> 02:01:27.893
our deal with Enbridge. Today you can all be ambassadors

02:01:27.949 --> 02:01:31.397
for radical urban climate action. Vote in favor of all

02:01:31.421 --> 02:01:35.253
of the recommendations concerning the Enbridge franchise agreement.

02:01:35.389 --> 02:01:38.989
Vote against big oil and gas, and vote for bold climate

02:01:39.077 --> 02:01:42.941
action. Be the international climate leaders that GCOM says

02:01:42.973 --> 02:01:46.773
you are. We only have five years until 2030

02:01:46.949 --> 02:01:48.105
for any chance.

